    "created_at": "2026-04-09T08:42:43Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-04-09T08:49:59Z",
    "body": "Given `<select>` now has a popover in appearance base, for which it is not possible to turn into a hint, I think we should allow nested autos to be demoted to hints. Therefore **option 3** is my vote.\n\nI think demoting to hint somewhat makes sense, I think the behaviour naturally flows out (in that there's a reasonable default answer for a lot of the questions that come out of that decision). I also think option 3 aligns with priority of constituencies, in that the browser is doing its best to recover from a developer error, meaning that the user actually gets something, rather than nothing.\n\nI think throwing is problematic as the best we can do on declarative instantiation is `console.error`. Some developers don't really check the console (or the console is now so noisy that signal/noise ratio is past the precipice), so those messages don't necessarily have quite the impact I think we might believe they do. I've paired with developers who've missed console messages because the staging/development environment is too noisy, or they don't have it open, or they simply filter the console window to try and get the noise down.\n\nThere are many instances of \"developers shouldn't be building it that way\" - my worry is that can be conflated with \"developers won't be building it that way\" or even that \"developers\" is a singular person or team. In some orgs the person who manages the button might be a different person (or in a different team) to the person who manages the popover, which is different to the team managing the parent popover, and so these things may break in ways which are unexpected - shifting like tectonic plates.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
