    "created_at": "2026-04-15T15:35:33Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-04-15T15:35:33Z",
    "body": "Sorry for the delay. So I was in the option-1 camp until I read @keithamus 's [comment](https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/12346#issuecomment-4212820062) above, which kind of convinced me that perhaps option-3 is the right answer here. I'm still a bit concerned that it will increase the complexity of the algorithms a bit, since we can no longer \"trust\" the attribute value to tell us what kind of popover it is. But I'm convinced by the real-sounding use case of a customizable-`<select>` inside a `popover=hint`. I don't think we should disallow that, since the fix for developers there is fairly harsh - convert the `hint` to an `auto`, etc.\n\nI think it might be going too far to just [remove the restriction](https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/12346#issuecomment-4209105575) entirely, since that really complicates the algorithms, and I'm not sure it's a net benefit to developers. I'm certainly not in favor of switching customizable-`<select>` to `popover=manual` because I do think the picker should play very nicely with other `popover=auto`'s.\n\nHow about we just store a bit on each popover that keeps track of whether it's an \"auto demoted to hint\", and work that into the existing algorithms that look for hint popovers? That would seem to fix the known use case of `<select>` inside `hint`, and shouldn't involve too much collateral damage, I think. ",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
